STEPS=[
 [r'(a) Both denominators are nonzero at x = 4. Substitute: \(3/4+1/(4-5)=3/4-1=-1/4\).',r'(b) The denominator at 1 is 4. Substitute and square: \(((1+3+2)/(1+1+2))^2=(6/4)^2=9/4\).',r'(c) Multiply by the conjugate. For x ≠ 1, \((\sqrt{x}-1)/(x-1)=1/(\sqrt{x}+1)\), whose limit is 1/2.',r'(d) Combine the numerator: \((1/x-1)/(x-1)=(1-x)/(x(x-1))=-1/x\) for x ≠ 1. Its limit is −1.',r'(e) Direct substitution is valid since cos²0 = 1. The limit is (1 − 0)/1 = 1.',r'(f) Write \(\tan x/x=(\sin x/x)/\cos x\). In radians, the two factors tend to 1 and 1, so the quotient tends to 1.',r'(g) For x ≠ 0 near 0, \((\sec x-1)/(x\sec x)=(1-\cos x)/x\). Rationalize with 1 + cos x to get \((\sin x/x)\sin x/(1+\cos x)\), which tends to 0.',r'(h) Write \(\sin^2(7x)/(7x)=[\sin(7x)/(7x)]\sin(7x)\). The factors tend to 1 and 0, so the limit is 0.'],
 ['(a) For x ≠ 4 the squared denominator is positive, and tends to 0 from both sides. Therefore −6/(x − 4)² tends to −∞; it has no finite real limit.',r'(b) Combine fractions: \((1/(x+3)-1/3)/x=[-x/(3(x+3))]/x=-1/(3(x+3))\) for x ≠ 0. Let x → 0 to obtain −1/9.',r'(c) Rationalize: \((\sqrt{x}-\sqrt{5})/(x-5)=1/(\sqrt{x}+\sqrt{5})\) for x ≠ 5. Its limit is \(1/(2\sqrt{5})\).'],
 [r'(a) Divide by n: \(5n/(n+7)=5/(1+7/n)\). Since 7/n → 0, the limit is 5.',r'(b) Divide by n: \((4-7n)/(8+n)=(4/n-7)/(8/n+1)\). The limit is −7.',r'(c) The terms alternate in sign, but \(-1/n^2\leq(-1)^n/n^2\leq1/n^2\). Both bounds tend to 0; the sandwich theorem gives limit 0.'],
 [r'(a) Substitute week 5: \(S(5)=5000+3600/7=38600/7\), approximately Rs 5514.29.', '(b) S is continuous at 5 because its denominator is 7 ≠ 0. Thus limₓ→5 S(x) = S(5) = 38600/7, approximately Rs 5514.29.', '(c) The source asks x → 16. The denominator is 18, so the limit is 5000 + 3600/18 = 5200 rupees.'],
 [],
 [r'(a) Treat x as fixed and positive. Rationalize: \((\sqrt{x+h}-\sqrt{x})/h=1/(\sqrt{x+h}+\sqrt{x})\) for h ≠ 0. Let h → 0 to obtain \(1/(2\sqrt{x})\). At x = 0, only h → 0⁺ is real and the quotient tends to +∞.',r'(b) For positive integers n, m and a ≠ 0, divide numerator and denominator by x − a. Their limits are \(na^{n-1}\) and \(ma^{m-1}\), so the ratio tends to \((n/m)a^{n-m}\). At a = 0 the ratio is xⁿ⁻ᵐ: its limit is 0 if n > m, 1 if n = m; if n < m it has no finite limit (even difference gives +∞; odd difference gives opposite one-sided infinities).',r'(c) Assume q ≠ 0. Use \(1-\cos u=2\sin^2(u/2)\). For p ≠ 0, the ratio becomes \((p/q)^2[\sin(p\theta/2)/(p\theta/2)]^2/[\sin(q\theta/2)/(q\theta/2)]^2\), whose limit is p²/q². For p = 0 it is 0. If q = 0 the original denominator is identically zero.',r'(d) Rewrite \((\tan\theta-\sin\theta)/\sin^3\theta=(1-\cos\theta)/(\cos\theta\sin^2\theta)=1/[\cos\theta(1+\cos\theta)]\) near 0, excluding 0. The limit is 1/2.',r'(e) Taking logs gives \(n\ln(1-1/n)=\ln(1-u)/u\) with u = 1/n → 0⁺. Since ln(1 − u)/u → −1, the log tends to −1. The original limit is e⁻¹.',r'(f) Put u = 2x². Then \((1+2x^2)^{1/x^2}=[(1+u)^{1/u}]^2\). As x → 0, u → 0⁺, so the limit is e².',r'(g) Rewrite \((x/(1+x))^x=(1+1/x)^{-x}=1/[(1+1/x)^x]\). As x → ∞, the denominator tends to e; the limit is e⁻¹.'],
 ['(i) Read the roof outline and the printed formula from the original crop. The two straight roof slopes meeting at a peak suggest an inverted absolute-value graph, equivalently two affine pieces.', '(ii) The displayed expression has a squared x-shift with a negative coefficient. It is a quadratic function with a downward-opening parabola.', 'These classifications use the visible shapes and formula structure. The exact coefficients of the first printed formula are not reliably legible, so its full algebraic model remains unverified. Do not treat the whole photographed object as the graph.']
]
